\section{Why there is a residual}{

Each EPV channel is \emph{chain EPV plus a linear box-score sum}. The box-score
half decomposes exactly, because every term is \code{count * weight}. The chain
half — the disposer/receiver split, the difficulty split and contest credit —
does not have a per-category form without instrumenting the credit path, so it
is reported whole, per channel, as \code{chain}.

\strong{The residual is a finding, not a leftover.} Its size relative to a player's
total says how much of his rating comes from context rather than counting
stats, which is exactly the thing a counting-stat profile page cannot show.
}

\section{What this is not}{

It is \strong{not} a sum of \code{delta_epv} by description. That was the obvious
approach and it is wrong: summing pbp's per-row \code{delta_epv} by player
correlates only \strong{0.626} with published EPV and is out by a mean of \strong{4.82
points per player-game}, because \code{delta_epv} is the swing \emph{caused by} an
event while the credit model splits it between disposer and receiver. Anything
built that way is a different statistic wearing the same name.
}

